[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329806 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17028eac-4ec5-5412-a437-179e2341db73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327136} {:answer/id #uuid "699925f0-6e8c-5f1a-b77d-e1c6f323fb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327842} {:answer/id #uuid "aa0370f2-56b3-5cc8-a52d-9b4a98865995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328406} {:answer/id #uuid "cdfe3a0e-970e-5bb9-8a35-e018230647f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328745} {:answer/id #uuid "f5621511-1f8d-5799-9acc-c62ea19974de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdfe3a0e-970e-5bb9-8a35-e018230647f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328745} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f990210-b8a8-5e34-9486-0ad7ae987df5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "66cb54a9-7be0-554a-a6fe-eb1aaa1b6f8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-47073" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x-8}=$" :question/worked-solution "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x-8} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{3x}\\cdot {2}^{-8} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{({2}^{3})}^{x}\\cdot {2}^{-8} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{8}^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{256}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{256})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{8}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={8}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/85/85319605a7c7b57d4c7ed29b8d409e3169f3331775580b91b70e2be99fef7986.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{8}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{256})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{8}^{x}=(\\frac{1}{256})\\cdot 0=0\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13c4946f-7cfd-5eea-9b2a-3c68b8c04bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451423} {:answer/id #uuid "631df741-fcdf-5ee9-b554-6ce847f8201f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "631df741-fcdf-5ee9-b554-6ce847f8201f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451424} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae7393b-6d61-58de-92a9-e49a25b03a56" :answer-field/key "Its domain is $-\\infty <x<\\infty $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452559} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec9a444e-8f67-59aa-984d-88ba3aa4a432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451425} {:answer/id #uuid "c1829ff3-c9af-5728-b060-4f94a8c1209f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1829ff3-c9af-5728-b060-4f94a8c1209f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451426} :answer-field/id #uuid "fac9daa5-4d61-5666-831a-625724e0a0cc" :answer-field/key "It's a decreasing function" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452560} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59f4be26-745f-500a-8014-e08a471c0a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451427} {:answer/id #uuid "d66a88ac-8487-5227-85df-df58a16cb930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59f4be26-745f-500a-8014-e08a471c0a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451427} :answer-field/id #uuid "55a523b7-faa6-5772-a250-4264622204cb" :answer-field/key "$f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f25b7dd1-f51b-52ea-a0c4-08f9ed823c0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-47747" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/28/288adf3d1da5cf4bb6e2530318aeb083b69d43639de7704f9df15672be477d0a.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement “Its domain is $-\\infty <x<\\infty $” is true. The function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}$ is defined for all input values $x.$\n\n- Statement “It's a decreasing function” is true. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $” is false. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements “Its domain is $-\\infty <x<\\infty $” and “It's a decreasing function” are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93deb04d-8a9a-580a-bf1d-6f1cfdb10031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328224} {:answer/id #uuid "aea64a1f-2fed-5288-956b-5b5778b99904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328451} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ed9b52-cf2e-5350-bf43-ad324db344a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328535} {:answer/id #uuid "b908e50c-2bef-5858-90e4-e63a0891cc59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328547} {:answer/id #uuid "db240cee-556f-5a6d-b1e9-7cc59058102c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db240cee-556f-5a6d-b1e9-7cc59058102c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328849} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e34a4ae-75c3-5ed8-ba03-8bbd3a0772e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d86d6bd5-c21f-569c-8d53-db55c08711d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-4776" :question/problem "For $x>0,$ find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{-4}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u=-{x}^{2}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{-4}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{-4}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit $u$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of the definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{-4}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(-{\\int }_{-4}^{u}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{-4}^{u}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{-4}^{u}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{u}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{x}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{u}) \\\\  & =-2x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{-{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-2x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12760dce-da89-5291-a8a0-2c55c0bba39c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327090} {:answer/id #uuid "5670dd9b-7642-5c17-bf53-74fc6017bee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$, $g(x)$ and $h(x)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327682} {:answer/id #uuid "618633e8-e3f6-5116-86ec-c30b48af2e69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327787} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7e2262-4cac-5940-9f49-685d19667975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327902} {:answer/id #uuid "9a03e921-c6b9-54ee-b1e2-24bc8d23f486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ and $h(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "618633e8-e3f6-5116-86ec-c30b48af2e69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327787} :answer-field/id #uuid "fff65d75-0460-569b-8f67-0d5ae1fa65fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0fbd6283-a30f-5fa2-ae4b-af58ef2f415f" :question/math-academy-id "q-48114" :question/problem "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=0?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ 0, & x=0,\\end{aligned} \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ 2, & x=0,\\end{aligned} \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}3, & x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+4}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For $y=f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(0)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}({x}^{2}-3x)={0}^{2}-3(0)=0, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}({x}^{2}-3x)={0}^{2}-3(0)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0=f(0).\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nNow, for $y=g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(0)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1})=\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+1}=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1})=\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}g(x)=1.$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\ng(0)=2\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,0}g(x).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$\n\nFinally, for $y=h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(0)=\\sqrt{0+4}=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}(3)=3\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}(\\sqrt{x+4})=\\sqrt{0+4}=2.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal.$\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22e53f34-1f08-55ea-b027-91a6570c9ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327234} {:answer/id #uuid "9d3be258-9fcf-5d57-a47f-1395e89bbde9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328293} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c08959-081a-5f50-a3f0-ce4b51612304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328658} {:answer/id #uuid "e95dd2da-108e-5550-b9b6-a61109df65bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328979} {:answer/id #uuid "efde6b46-482d-55c0-a8b0-e2ad91b34126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22e53f34-1f08-55ea-b027-91a6570c9ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327234} :answer-field/id #uuid "23c86c24-0972-56ca-8779-29609bc15897" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf60d52b-f79d-5da2-9400-715a7866a23a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48504" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{ln⁡(x)},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=e.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=ln⁡x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot ln⁡x-{x}^{2}\\cdot (ln⁡x)^{′}}{(ln⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\cdot ln⁡(x)-{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x})}{{(ln⁡(x))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\cdot ln⁡(x)-x}{{ln}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(2ln⁡(x)-1)}{{ln}^{2}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=e$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(e) & =\\frac{e(2ln⁡(e)-1)}{{ln}^{2}⁡(e)} \\\\  & =\\frac{e(2-1)}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $e.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329889 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45e2c946-cebd-5416-8a76-6327b5030505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327546} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9341d4-99b6-50f0-aa6d-9c6dd9382020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328140} {:answer/id #uuid "acbeb3ba-53eb-5b9a-b551-835bc36de883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328432} {:answer/id #uuid "ca6bee3f-bd8c-5dcf-8359-5ca512f57072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328708} {:answer/id #uuid "db84f8eb-8423-54a1-b09a-ae141285ed5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db84f8eb-8423-54a1-b09a-ae141285ed5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328851} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac2d96d1-6187-53ac-b42b-8e94f57a3b62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "33385857-91b0-513f-9a43-e506cd0cb163" :question/math-academy-id "q-48512" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\pi -3x)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is given by ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,u=2\\pi -3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(-{csc}^{2}⁡u)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi -3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =3(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ y & =3x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329893 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "384338bf-f318-5d61-8c1b-529fb0234472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327424} {:answer/id #uuid "7c5be1ae-ca65-575b-b9c7-434a5c372b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328004} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9876aa-d0a3-59b8-abf7-ccd7d1ed3f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328589} {:answer/id #uuid "c4fb3ad6-5f57-5c3c-8c5b-af3bfe56b412" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328662} {:answer/id #uuid "d3486b55-343d-574d-841a-f82bc6da521d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd9876aa-d0a3-59b8-abf7-ccd7d1ed3f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328589} :answer-field/id #uuid "112aeaa5-dec9-5e9a-985f-80f67a857f64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ab02bbb-b5d3-5bdb-bab1-8d85b37d2387" :question/math-academy-id "q-48520" :question/problem "The radius of a circle is increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the circle is numerically equal to two-thirds of the rate of increase of the circumference. What is the radius of the circle at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $C$ the circumference of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ C & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $C=2\\pi r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi r & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "574f873d-1eb4-5105-b7e4-2707745365b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327695} {:answer/id #uuid "72aa071b-03ed-54ed-ab39-80379736a889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327927} {:answer/id #uuid "dc947e82-8d8c-5904-b822-6abde22ab204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328861} {:answer/id #uuid "dd56ec3a-109f-5a54-a6a4-2b1a9207588d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328869} {:answer/id #uuid "f496029a-484d-5c86-957c-d28ddc29fef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "574f873d-1eb4-5105-b7e4-2707745365b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327695} :answer-field/id #uuid "42aa65f6-1182-5596-b478-99b5b41b00db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c37401d-7602-5cd3-a2b5-e1b454a59944" :question/math-academy-id "q-48523" :question/problem "The radius of a circle is increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the circle is numerically equal to one third of the rate of increase of the diameter. Determine the radius of the circle at that moment." :question/worked-solution "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $D$ the diameter of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the diameter $D$ of the circle. Since $D=2r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $D$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{D}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}D}(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ D & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $D=2r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2r & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cd88001-594a-5d1f-b80b-a2b258386cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{ln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327751} {:answer/id #uuid "9a80f11d-085c-5ea7-ab96-6ce21f491dc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3ln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328270} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee8c8de-94f2-593c-9518-5dde2376aefa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5ln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328306} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd2202c-ab6e-5f06-8a86-6652d3d0e787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{ln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328731} {:answer/id #uuid "db9b7a29-e7bc-51c7-a0e1-df492323b2af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{ln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccd2202c-ab6e-5f06-8a86-6652d3d0e787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{ln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328731} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a14bf32-63c1-5362-83c1-dfaf55039036" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e13df585-bbb8-5747-9099-5dc67c6a041d" :question/math-academy-id "q-48656" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y={log}_{3}⁡(5x-2)+5$ at the point where $x=\\frac{7}{15}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{3}⁡(u)+5,\\,u=5x-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(3)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(3)}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5x-2)ln⁡(3)}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(5x-2)ln⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=\\frac{7}{15}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=7/15} & =\\frac{5}{(5x-2)ln⁡(3)}{|}_{x=7/15} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(5(\\frac{7}{15})-2)ln⁡(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\frac{1}{3}ln⁡(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{ln⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329910 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23c0c48e-731d-5863-8977-745ca9f611af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327245} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef944fc-3b12-512e-9e2e-62f784e7af4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$15$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328181} {:answer/id #uuid "9c209fb0-48e4-59f2-a016-5ea6eddd4dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328283} {:answer/id #uuid "b616b2da-2c7d-56fa-a950-f6daba793a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328517} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a0fa62-0f4e-55ea-a496-546cea36c23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2a0fa62-0f4e-55ea-a496-546cea36c23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328635} :answer-field/id #uuid "7676a5ad-5930-54f7-bb2b-9e2785aaf584" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "95af6bee-d5fd-511a-9bf5-4cbc8b987124" :question/math-academy-id "q-48701" :question/problem "The distance $d$, in meters, that a car has traveled after $t$ seconds can be modeled by the function $d(t)=\\frac{2{t}^{3}+7t}{t+1}.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change of the distance traveled when $t=2$ seconds?" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the distance traveled when $t=2$ seconds is equal to ${d}^{′}(2).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2{t}^{3}+7t}{t+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(6{t}^{2}+7)(t+1)-(2{t}^{3}+7t)(1)}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}+7}{(t+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{4(2)^{3}+6(2)^{2}+7}{((2)+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32+24+7}{9} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the distance traveled when $t=2$ seconds is $7$ meters per second."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0232a753-029f-5bc7-a6b8-6d7eac821b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$s(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡s+1)$ squared millimeters per centimeter" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326956} {:answer/id #uuid "02cc2928-9aea-59ad-81aa-60c12396698b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡s$ squared millimeters per centimeter" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326968} {:answer/id #uuid "2232f388-1965-515d-8434-43897fac2b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\operatorname{ln}⁡s+1$ squared millimeters per centimeter" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327229} {:answer/id #uuid "53cb7095-26e2-51d4-9b11-8c13ec49c4f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$s(\\operatorname{ln}⁡s+2)$ squared millimeters per centimeter" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327660} {:answer/id #uuid "8a8902f4-ec4e-5f32-bde2-6cf1eaa6bf7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${s}^{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡s-1)$ squared millimeters per centimeter" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0232a753-029f-5bc7-a6b8-6d7eac821b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$s(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡s+1)$ squared millimeters per centimeter" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326956} :answer-field/id #uuid "490b96eb-d62a-5de6-8c23-aeb0a9c70545" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aa5612ea-ff70-5171-a461-d7cdeed45cdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-48716" :question/problem "The function $L(s)={s}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡s$ models the surface area of a flower's petal, in squared millimeters, as the flower's stem grows to a length of $s$ centimeters, where $s>1.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change of the surface area as a function of $s?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}({s}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡s) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}({s}^{2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡s+{s}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡s) \\\\  & =(2s)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡s+{s}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{s}) \\\\  & =2s\\operatorname{ln}⁡s+s \\\\  & =s(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡s+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the surface area is $s(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡s+1)$ squared millimeters per centimeter."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329922 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67e970de-f69a-5faf-a1f9-894ed9152874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{4}{3}$ gallons per dollar" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327833} {:answer/id #uuid "8f9408c5-73a6-5a07-a318-c5f403503efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{3}$ dollars per gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328188} {:answer/id #uuid "9ade8d73-66b2-5f69-b283-68aec2a496fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{3}{4}$ dollars per gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328275} {:answer/id #uuid "c1834f7c-5892-5dbc-a7c9-15c6c63601cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{4}{3}$ dollars per gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328623} {:answer/id #uuid "e1cff52d-84f6-52a6-8958-91f1bdf338cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{3}{4}$ gallons per dollar" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1834f7c-5892-5dbc-a7c9-15c6c63601cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{4}{3}$ dollars per gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328623} :answer-field/id #uuid "888f3e42-3c7e-547b-8e3c-866522e779ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "113af4b2-44b6-5fbb-b225-f94f6ccbc4f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-48742" :question/problem "The function $P(v)=\\frac{2v+50}{v+1}$ models the price at which gas is sold, in dollars, depending on the volume $v$ sold the previous day, in gallons. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the price of gas when $v=5$ gallons?" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the price when $v=5$ gallons is equal to ${P}^{′}(5).$\n\nNow, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(v) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}v}(\\frac{2v+50}{v+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}v}(2v+50)\\cdot (v+1)-(2v+50)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}v}(v+1)}{(v+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)\\cdot (v+1)-(2v+50)\\cdot (1)}{(v+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2v+2-2v-50}{(v+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{48}{(v+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $v=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(5) & =-\\frac{48}{((5)+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{48}{36} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the price when $v=5$ gallons is $-\\frac{4}{3}$ dollars per gallon."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f3a6d29-8447-54ea-b633-1d7cce96f049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327356} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa152c1-ba8a-54d1-beef-df5952e43e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327986} {:answer/id #uuid "abd40024-14c1-5370-bca5-2f3720f55d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328422} {:answer/id #uuid "f63fe888-fda4-52a2-90fe-09222ddcb3a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329094} {:answer/id #uuid "fbaa3c62-a3e8-5ace-b78d-c07a5d68afc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aa152c1-ba8a-54d1-beef-df5952e43e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327986} :answer-field/id #uuid "48143dd4-41bc-5843-9821-2be1c03ec34a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3736415b-c933-532e-bf24-6ad834dc5a47" :question/math-academy-id "q-48843" :question/problem "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(1)=8,\\,{f}^{′}(1)=6,\\,g(1)=4,\\,{g}^{′}(1)=4\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})$ at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)g(x)-f(x){g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=1$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})|}_{x=1} & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)g(1)-f(1){g}^{′}(1)}{{g}^{2}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 4-8\\cdot 4}{{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e67f9c6-bd51-533a-bed4-bda0a67507a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327057} {:answer/id #uuid "38cff357-fbb4-555f-a0e9-eb41138d7564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi <x<-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327433} {:answer/id #uuid "54a5c36b-2112-58c9-b40d-9bbacbd5a314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}<x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327666} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7cfa1d-6f7a-5483-afce-a52986329c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi <x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328032} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8eaef2-0161-5f1a-ac38-040d88eb492f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi <x<\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f7cfa1d-6f7a-5483-afce-a52986329c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi <x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328032} :answer-field/id #uuid "859970f9-e96a-5e5a-8a28-1c14c08438d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ca4ba403-bc07-505e-b911-fafd2598ba3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-48855" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})$ is defined for $x\\in (-2\\pi ,0),$ find the values of $x$ for which the function is strictly increasing." :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain is $x\\in (-2\\pi ,0),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi  & <x<0 \\\\ -\\pi  & <\\frac{x}{2}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that cosine is positive in the first and the fourth quadrant. So, this reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & <\\frac{x}{2}<0 \\\\ -\\pi  & <x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)$ is strictly increasing on the interval $-\\pi <x<0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13445d14-3cdd-563f-82ec-b2491e818710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327098} {:answer/id #uuid "37a4fac9-0c61-5de6-ab84-a4c2af83f366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327417} {:answer/id #uuid "59d3bd71-26f7-5f6f-83a8-8dfd424280fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327722} {:answer/id #uuid "8c242195-ddbd-5be0-a37f-68cd9d74f6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328135} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6ab67a-9e24-5695-b84b-cb14c7525adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37a4fac9-0c61-5de6-ab84-a4c2af83f366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327417} :answer-field/id #uuid "332afad3-ac34-5e61-9a48-c791fe402945" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb6dd170-3acf-530d-b722-eed271826bd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-48922" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+3}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+3)-(x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+3)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+3)-(x+1)\\cdot (2x)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+3-2{x}^{2}-2x}{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-2x-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3-2x-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 3-2x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-3 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-3$ and $x=1.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-3$ and $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a703046-a85a-5ece-a983-708c0b8d6f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327983} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ee6fe2-d9b6-5f50-b4ab-0cded00cec6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328475} {:answer/id #uuid "c87f5e43-c99a-5e69-b2a8-4b1acd7f66e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328693} {:answer/id #uuid "da4456ff-e9bf-59da-b1da-79d48eda22f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328843} {:answer/id #uuid "fc835340-7d74-544d-9355-7ce9dc98615e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a703046-a85a-5ece-a983-708c0b8d6f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327983} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba18193e-ff57-5a29-ac86-b2eea500b525" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "01449f90-92ea-536a-a2b8-0c9b226cdccd" :question/math-academy-id "q-48949" :question/problem "![](images/54/54c2987e0078899132d5a350a87e0e1283bd919e8fbe065b86574c2df2348e2f.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(3)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(3)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(3)\\cdot g(3)-f(3)\\cdot {g}^{′}(3)}{(g(3))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(3)=-1$ and $g(3)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (2,5)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(3)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (1,\\frac{7}{2})$ equals $1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(3)=1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(3)\\cdot g(3)-f(3)\\cdot {g}^{′}(3)}{{(g(3))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-(-1)\\cdot 1}{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329942 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e62398c-c7f2-5a90-94bd-6534d22dd4f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328169} {:answer/id #uuid "99b31e0c-b9b1-53de-af5e-060da1733789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328262} {:answer/id #uuid "a8518df8-be72-56e5-a662-44b7d46a18e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328392} {:answer/id #uuid "dc886477-bb24-5eb3-b05d-d4dc07641d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328859} {:answer/id #uuid "ff6183e1-ed84-51b0-b43f-2a95ee508630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8518df8-be72-56e5-a662-44b7d46a18e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328392} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef80b9f7-4df8-5e89-9334-106d6c34b0e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "432a3453-fc74-5b9b-8b50-79e9585f1cfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-48952" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e13e109301fca50036cbf933b2a7946f3f53397f39f5e249c0d64e1f6ae78ba2.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${q}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})$ where $q(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})=\\frac{{f}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot g(\\frac{3}{2})-f(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot {g}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})}{{(g(\\frac{3}{2}))}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(\\frac{3}{2})=1$ and $g(\\frac{3}{2})=-1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $0.$ So, ${f}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})=0.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})=1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot g(\\frac{3}{2})-f(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot {g}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})}{{(g(\\frac{3}{2}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03c9c5af-ae60-5c19-98de-2006a010f6a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326974} {:answer/id #uuid "725259ac-e334-586e-b4bf-e101625ee9dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327923} {:answer/id #uuid "b72f49d7-33bf-5349-9709-3d90489d4014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328527} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a0ce48-bfd5-5bc5-a291-7a5731068d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328553} {:answer/id #uuid "e911496f-9a62-5fc4-92e0-55083c62392c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03c9c5af-ae60-5c19-98de-2006a010f6a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326974} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc3899ec-04b9-5456-8803-fb530c320aa6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "963ec6dc-aa96-5129-a681-815db2627be7" :question/math-academy-id "q-48960" :question/problem "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=3$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})$ at $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)g(x)-f(x){g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=3$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})|}_{x=3} & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(3)g(3)-f(3){g}^{′}(3)}{(g(3))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4)\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329945 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e8612e1-4306-5c49-8f06-ea7b5746fb9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327198} {:answer/id #uuid "6e07c738-ae4e-5929-8e41-f604a66d3b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327887} {:answer/id #uuid "a079bd8b-5910-56b6-8f1c-d4c339443dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328323} {:answer/id #uuid "b0b17434-b7e6-5437-8e57-734e2d888aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328467} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d7b11d-8f56-571b-a255-73e3d45babfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a079bd8b-5910-56b6-8f1c-d4c339443dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328323} :answer-field/id #uuid "79b8edb2-ee82-5b3e-a449-9f7e35912b1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d3a263d5-d91f-5cc8-85f3-bb4b472cc091" :question/math-academy-id "q-48965" :question/problem "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(2)=-1,\\,{f}^{′}(2)=-2,\\,g(2)=-5,\\,{g}^{′}(2)=1\n$$\n\nIf $h(x)=\\frac{5f(x)+4}{g(x)+6},$ find the value of ${h}^{′}(2).$" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{u}{v})=\\frac{v{u}^{′}-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5f(x)+4$ and $v=g(x)+6.$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the quotient rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{v{u}^{′}-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(g(x)+6)\\cdot 5{f}^{′}(x)-(5f(x)+4)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x)+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{f}^{′}(x)(g(x)+6)-{g}^{′}(x)(5f(x)+4)}{(g(x)+6)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=2$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{5{f}^{′}(2)(g(2)+6)-{g}^{′}(2)(5f(2)+4)}{(g(2)+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-5+6)-1\\cdot (5\\cdot (-1)+4)}{(-5+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-10)\\cdot 1-(-1)}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83244f3f-d4c0-5400-b575-23be5005ae8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331114} {:answer/id #uuid "af9bdfa5-e153-5f0e-9579-128cbb842449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{sec}^{2}⁡3x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331502} {:answer/id #uuid "f2b54574-c597-5d2e-b623-f716b57a4a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332087} {:answer/id #uuid "faa172fc-7c4f-5556-9b0f-aa9ceeebb1f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332150} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfd483a-4f6a-531f-b6bb-82a660adeb6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbfd483a-4f6a-531f-b6bb-82a660adeb6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332164} :answer-field/id #uuid "34d0ff93-0be5-5775-8d8d-a9a9d7becf1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80f918c1-b02c-51df-b3e3-2c8413bacd10" :question/math-academy-id "q-49256" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡3x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int {sec}^{2}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=3x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48b77ad6-2790-5fb9-ac75-c531ab004c02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330601} {:answer/id #uuid "8a571c79-fa20-5257-bd99-b3a3310b2bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331178} {:answer/id #uuid "8b31c3cf-dd75-5df7-9b83-aa025a1ed598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331185} {:answer/id #uuid "9549f7ba-8509-55bb-ac17-34f5f78e130c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331274} {:answer/id #uuid "d75920df-cc3f-5f99-8620-29d4802abe07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48b77ad6-2790-5fb9-ac75-c531ab004c02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330601} :answer-field/id #uuid "075617b4-7fd7-5fe0-bba2-6157b32a856f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a78f750a-573a-57da-b09b-e012074904df" :question/math-academy-id "q-49284" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe use the fact that $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11dbbe25-f605-5318-ad46-dc495bb13c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330105} {:answer/id #uuid "ab70ca2b-b076-587e-a88e-1d10fb8a7498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331469} {:answer/id #uuid "bf37637a-7683-56c4-bb69-925e9cc147a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331645} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d35bf0-51c8-56cd-8c8d-1bcbce77b9af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331802} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ef6fe2-0740-5343-acd8-b64c29efd3d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11dbbe25-f605-5318-ad46-dc495bb13c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330105} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0499ae3-ea01-5507-9906-ec2b8d72e827" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "489c69ce-d415-5c5f-8b91-c246b0512a1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-49294" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcc78643dd44633f778d954de32a955dc5803abf7dc059c30bbbda97bc35413f.png)\n\nA region in the $xy$-plane is bounded by the curve $y=2{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\pi ,$ as shown above. The area of the region is" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(2{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nRecall  ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1,$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nHence, the area of the region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }[\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)+1]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+3x)|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi +3\\pi )-(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\pi +\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{9\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "387deba6-304f-5fb3-b47c-69364ee68fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330456} {:answer/id #uuid "9dbd38f5-b598-5f41-b8e7-6a2e96e2e92b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331363} {:answer/id #uuid "d53e8abf-0959-5a9c-839c-8502bcc230cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331839} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d621ba-aeb4-5686-b23c-d3e8132c191b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332066} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f51ac7-9c9e-5857-be9f-ae4d9ff5c99e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0d621ba-aeb4-5686-b23c-d3e8132c191b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332066} :answer-field/id #uuid "650973e6-7b74-5955-b3e6-7ccc008e872e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "22c49d6a-442c-592d-b81d-9330777b0cac" :question/math-academy-id "q-49325" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{3(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We are using the identity\n\n$$\n{tan}^{2}⁡x+1={sec}^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{3(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2-3\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =6-2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23c4acae-b369-5f9b-8ab6-963eb8877cae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330257} {:answer/id #uuid "33963c3d-8e28-5e8a-8a4d-093fcb4cb9f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330407} {:answer/id #uuid "3f105bea-c32e-589f-8c75-66f47fa62806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330509} {:answer/id #uuid "ad881229-1a77-57ac-84f9-baa044032197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331487} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d64f50-21be-5975-8c1b-ab28d7fb0be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{3}{16}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0d64f50-21be-5975-8c1b-ab28d7fb0be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{3}{16}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331659} :answer-field/id #uuid "5093329b-485c-593c-9a8f-8d21ad28ebee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7806e71e-9cb7-55be-836c-04e6fc97f767" :question/math-academy-id "q-49357" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{x+1}{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $4$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x+1}{4(x+\\frac{1}{4})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{x+1}{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4} & 1 & 1 \\\\  & ↓ & -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & 1 & \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{(x+\\frac{1}{4})} & =1+\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4})}{(x+\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{3}{4(x+\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{3}{4x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int (1+\\frac{3}{4x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\int \\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[x+\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x+1|]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{3}{16}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332725 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "254e5cf7-471f-50e8-be6a-00fe725a2c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330269} {:answer/id #uuid "33951d1f-ea38-594d-9f46-2002b6dbb06c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330406} {:answer/id #uuid "b174efff-e3c2-5199-bf28-70379f6c34c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331519} {:answer/id #uuid "c16c5cbe-2009-58f3-9c63-acc020011ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331667} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a19484-5637-5ad4-bd83-dd37bd740e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33951d1f-ea38-594d-9f46-2002b6dbb06c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330406} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a8bfb5c-94b5-5347-9440-09e9286e0491" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a1457a43-fe5c-5c00-af78-30db339e55cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-49463" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{x+2}{2x-3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $2$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+2}{2x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x+2}{2(x-\\frac{3}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{x+2}{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & 1 & 2 \\\\  & ↓ & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & 1 & \\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{(x-\\frac{3}{2})} & =1+\\frac{(\\frac{7}{2})}{(x-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{7}{2(x-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{7}{2x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+2}{2x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (1+\\frac{7}{2x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\int \\text{d}x+7\\int \\frac{1}{2x-3}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[x+\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "338680db-24cd-505d-bb86-f7e20ca84bfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330404} {:answer/id #uuid "37b3e49f-6ca8-5bb1-b4c0-459a22bff976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330446} {:answer/id #uuid "64c0b8af-0611-5474-b601-c4d8f02c186c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330847} {:answer/id #uuid "6dceecd7-7163-532b-a02a-e72f91ff8016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330935} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c95368-b6b0-53bc-8846-e7aaa782680e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3c95368-b6b0-53bc-8846-e7aaa782680e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331418} :answer-field/id #uuid "92054451-6884-5082-bde2-ac31a363973e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ea190a7-8ce1-54ef-ac76-4af3dbfd44d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-49465" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{x+1}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $3$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x+1}{3(x+\\frac{2}{3})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{x+1}{(x+\\frac{2}{3})}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & 1 & 1 \\\\  & ↓ & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & 1 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{(x+\\frac{2}{3})} & =1+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3})}{(x+\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{3(x+\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{3x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int (1+\\frac{1}{3x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\int \\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[x+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "024f569b-84cc-57ef-8f4d-9dc8de0df792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329984} {:answer/id #uuid "12e1d549-32b5-52ba-8653-965154243838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330111} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd86b9e-f501-52cd-b1cc-9ff1facc3fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330482} {:answer/id #uuid "6428015d-361f-5b50-9364-a1602259dd12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330841} {:answer/id #uuid "fbaac824-d193-5f69-9c43-16185bc76ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "024f569b-84cc-57ef-8f4d-9dc8de0df792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329984} :answer-field/id #uuid "21cb126f-fb81-5a2d-8f46-32591eb308f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00f3edb2-19bb-54fa-b405-1ba68902442b" :question/math-academy-id "q-49505" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{4\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/4)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}.$ \n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (2\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332736 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24d0c2f0-e1ef-5f9d-b277-1d69053a1f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330265} {:answer/id #uuid "5d04e998-1dc4-5619-8be2-aa1e5c1b7c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330779} {:answer/id #uuid "866ac174-f329-56ce-b375-426c38e8089c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331143} {:answer/id #uuid "a72e2a87-1718-5692-ae75-612ad60536bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331438} {:answer/id #uuid "d944c1b0-1445-5a57-b346-1c4580f6e918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a72e2a87-1718-5692-ae75-612ad60536bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331438} :answer-field/id #uuid "7066a7e5-a4e7-571e-8102-812757ff24f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf21744c-e558-571b-b3ed-9b3f13019f23" :question/math-academy-id "q-49538" :question/problem "What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $4{x}^{2}=(2x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{1+(2x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332737 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46e86a4b-cc4c-5a6f-aaf3-ef2234d941ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330588} {:answer/id #uuid "569e6b60-bf68-55c0-a58d-f5683e2d1476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330724} {:answer/id #uuid "87600d1e-7d95-5096-a855-c3bc9ca0e904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331159} {:answer/id #uuid "9e6b1507-21a9-5241-8a35-d41f437e8d29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331368} {:answer/id #uuid "b73d0144-5e6d-5adf-89c2-88b202a8b0e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b73d0144-5e6d-5adf-89c2-88b202a8b0e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331568} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece6c44b-e4c3-57ea-8f7c-8273c4e5c5c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "edefe020-e961-5a43-95be-73f3210a7870" :question/math-academy-id "q-49546" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}}\\, & ={\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/25)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/25}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/5)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}}} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-5/2}^{5/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/5)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (5\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{-1/2}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22bf65d9-1368-5c83-bc06-2f787ae31aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330249} {:answer/id #uuid "b84605c3-6d64-59e4-a03f-63d799bb1439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(5x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331577} {:answer/id #uuid "c163d792-fcdb-58ce-be5c-05660a506b28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331666} {:answer/id #uuid "c187c878-5877-5b0a-ab41-952209abe59c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331668} {:answer/id #uuid "e20ac18c-ab7a-518f-8d37-1b59b1612d09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22bf65d9-1368-5c83-bc06-2f787ae31aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330249} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a6a8623-72fb-5f8a-9e38-396d560f8321" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73d1e60d-39c3-527b-ad50-26d30b66cdba" :question/math-academy-id "q-49550" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{25+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{25+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{25\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/25)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{25+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (5\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0218ebee-f46b-52a3-af27-002bd2e16fdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{8})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329977} {:answer/id #uuid "2d0a098d-fa78-5f98-bfe7-2e76cd1fe7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330349} {:answer/id #uuid "3e1c856a-d31e-5281-a2a6-f20cbee86a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(8x)}{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330499} {:answer/id #uuid "6da04d85-0964-58d1-bd80-e9e62694fa8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{8})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330930} {:answer/id #uuid "e57ddc07-4a02-59ea-b4b1-dd526c6b3d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{8})}{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6da04d85-0964-58d1-bd80-e9e62694fa8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{8})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330930} :answer-field/id #uuid "d448c3a1-6bd6-56fc-a1f7-e15ec5601f57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e79ee700-e1a2-5377-9595-991fdfcefa49" :question/math-academy-id "q-49551" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{64+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{64+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2}{64\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/64)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{64}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/64}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/8)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{8})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{8}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{8}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=8\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{64+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{32}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/8)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (8\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{8})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332743 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a0b1ce-6161-5e73-b36b-31d37e3b0b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4{(1-{y}^{4})}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330112} {:answer/id #uuid "279bd955-25ed-5deb-9e91-1b260848ad7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2{(1-{y}^{4})}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330302} {:answer/id #uuid "4482d3ef-e1bd-52a2-968c-377e9def4806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{(1-{y}^{4})}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330566} {:answer/id #uuid "95ddf6e6-b070-55fd-b7d5-d3c0133dcc47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{(1-{y}^{4})}^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331280} {:answer/id #uuid "b12013db-5592-5de5-ac15-a7f940470213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(1-{y}^{4})}^{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13a0b1ce-6161-5e73-b36b-31d37e3b0b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4{(1-{y}^{4})}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330112} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d27f773-d358-573a-9ebd-6ee1d5a4748e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "052c4300-130d-5380-bb49-cade415fdcf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-49561" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{4{y}^{3}}{{(1-{y}^{4})}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=1-{y}^{4}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=-4{y}^{3}\\,⟹\\,(-\\frac{1}{4})\\text{d}u={y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{y}^{3}}{{(1-{y}^{4})}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}y & =4\\int \\frac{1}{{(1-{y}^{4})}^{5}}\\cdot {y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{1}{4{u}^{4}})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{u}^{4}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{(1-{y}^{4})}^{4}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ef83982-261a-5377-ad53-2bc71ddfd687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{2}{25}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330947} {:answer/id #uuid "72bfff82-d9bc-576b-a1f9-1dfdf701ff8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{2}{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330982} {:answer/id #uuid "82b84591-7046-58b4-84d6-77913dfe2fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331106} {:answer/id #uuid "cdfe9d37-9b0f-5d20-9752-197af6970e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331776} {:answer/id #uuid "ef2f39c7-e1af-59ca-9806-10165e507215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82b84591-7046-58b4-84d6-77913dfe2fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331106} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0595bf1-06ee-55f0-8c25-ffa49679626d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e73ea18d-60c8-54bb-aacd-d18fc23ab422" :question/math-academy-id "q-49565" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3{(2)}^{n}}{{5}^{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{3{(2)}^{n}}{{5}^{n}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{n}}{{5}^{n}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with $r=\\frac{2}{5}.$ \n\nSince $|r|<1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332745 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01fdd384-a572-5724-9364-8acb408f2ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329976} {:answer/id #uuid "40ccecfa-a656-5109-b074-472b0a8ab1cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330526} {:answer/id #uuid "5a4aa74e-0e4c-5d1a-93ae-9302fd3645d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330757} {:answer/id #uuid "b994301b-7078-571a-b444-f5890625ea4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331590} {:answer/id #uuid "e6504bdb-2c3c-5430-a7f9-f5f85facc1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01fdd384-a572-5724-9364-8acb408f2ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329976} :answer-field/id #uuid "28c2b40b-2f58-56ca-bd6c-3ed7effb446f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "82a7ff27-052c-59e7-b715-3cb92743a1e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-49570" :question/problem "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n+3}}{{3}^{n}\\cdot x},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{n+3}}{{3}^{n}\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{n}\\cdot {x}^{3}}{{3}^{n}\\cdot x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{x}{3})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{x}{3}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,0<x\\le 3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332747 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088243d9-1f83-5d73-9676-61898c216f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(9-{x}^{2})}^{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330023} {:answer/id #uuid "19751e43-a723-581f-94f1-b9c5b12f13c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2{(9-{x}^{2})}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330169} {:answer/id #uuid "2ad52c65-7a01-580d-80a2-76c6aec15062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8{(9-{x}^{2})}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330330} {:answer/id #uuid "b3be71fe-ed7f-5ca0-9df0-61439e808dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{(9-{x}^{2})}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331538} {:answer/id #uuid "eb376da1-92a5-54a7-80f1-3352eec3f955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4{(9-{x}^{2})}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ad52c65-7a01-580d-80a2-76c6aec15062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8{(9-{x}^{2})}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330330} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a4767d5-1a9b-5c3a-b8d3-05782ae04071" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f49e34e0-20d0-57c8-b086-6444ff1aa1fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-49575" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{x}{(9-{x}^{2})^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=9-{x}^{2}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2x\\,⟹\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{(9-{x}^{2})}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{(9-{x}^{2})}^{5}}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{4{u}^{4}})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8{u}^{4}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8(9-{x}^{2})^{4}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "015efc40-0245-51b0-8584-9d8863daf2ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329965} {:answer/id #uuid "1d4f7919-d4be-5bc2-95ed-360d81517f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330205} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca64371-9faa-5fe4-8c93-0adb0568797f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331352} {:answer/id #uuid "a508c193-fc4a-5943-afde-92add946dd9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331429} {:answer/id #uuid "b48e7594-a266-5ab6-9fbd-f59ec50f663a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a508c193-fc4a-5943-afde-92add946dd9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331429} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6998351-d094-544b-b4bf-3ac3036d62aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b1638ebc-f057-5366-96fe-12ca453d0401" :question/math-academy-id "q-4961" :question/problem "The values of functions $h(x)$ and $z(x)$ and their derivatives at $t=2$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $h(t)$ | $z(t)$ | ${h}^{′}(t)$ | ${z}^{′}(t)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $5$ | $8$ | $-3$ | $4$ |\n\nIf $f(t)=\\frac{1-h(t)}{z(t)},$ compute ${f}^{′}(2).$" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{u}{v})=\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=1-h(t)$ and $v=z(t).$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=-{h}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={z}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the quotient rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-{h}^{′}(t))\\cdot z(t)-(1-h(t))\\cdot {z}^{′}(t)}{(z(t))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-z(t){h}^{′}(t)-{z}^{′}(t)(1-h(t))}{{z}^{2}(t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=2,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{-z(2){h}^{′}(2)-{z}^{′}(2)(1-h(2))}{{z}^{2}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\cdot (-3)-4\\cdot (1-5)}{{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24+16}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332757 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38443001-42f7-5354-910c-d2bda1d2489f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x-3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330451} {:answer/id #uuid "51f9d630-7059-5b2c-a570-244434b31bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330693} {:answer/id #uuid "80e7251c-9714-530e-bd0c-307cd40efb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{x-4}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331092} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8b59b8-3d3d-5312-b267-a0ef3e145541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x+4}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331597} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd6c1db-a953-5e1e-9e0c-ca534d859d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51f9d630-7059-5b2c-a570-244434b31bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330693} :answer-field/id #uuid "3810e1f1-bba5-549c-8da7-558d1cab6398" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a3d68b6-b7c9-51b5-a32f-dec5ef3543dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-49655" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7+6x-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7+6x-{x}^{2} & =-({x}^{2}-6x-7) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+[{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}]-7) \\\\  & =-((x-3)^{2}-16) \\\\  & =16-(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7+6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16-(x-3)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16(1-(x-3)^{2}/16)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-(x-3)^{2}/16}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{1-(x-3)^{2}/16}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((x-3)/4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{x-3}{4}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x-3}{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7+6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((x-3)/4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{4})+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d04d3d4-1dd0-57fb-b1e2-c225a604887a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330065} {:answer/id #uuid "10711776-d5af-58ee-a204-f0481a37ef0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330095} {:answer/id #uuid "94d2489f-97d4-5923-a4e5-c84c3ac1206a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331268} {:answer/id #uuid "bef58735-ea92-5021-ba86-331e3acbbeb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331643} {:answer/id #uuid "e88ab2f9-1ace-5545-9427-562efa040d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bef58735-ea92-5021-ba86-331e3acbbeb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331643} :answer-field/id #uuid "05dc3940-684e-5059-b3ec-b817a2dd3311" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b8bc1c4-8b9c-5e47-93e0-c4965c12347b" :question/math-academy-id "q-49669" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into  the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2x\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={-\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}|}_{0}^{\\pi /6}+\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}|}_{0}^{\\pi /6}+{\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}[\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & +\\frac{2}{9}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}[0-0]+\\frac{2}{9}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ac416bd-1ef2-598d-9769-0fc6af1898a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330183} {:answer/id #uuid "92f021d2-f657-568a-9458-97dc25ae2244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331255} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f49c5b-b91f-5372-945d-fd51d107045f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331524} {:answer/id #uuid "bc706a10-6660-59a2-b283-dfcba28564b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331611} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ff4fa6-edc2-533d-88c9-09406c898508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1f49c5b-b91f-5372-945d-fd51d107045f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331524} :answer-field/id #uuid "e116d325-1e59-5f3b-b1ef-e63b4cf776b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7d1b926-6342-572b-ba02-f2edcfbab3a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-49674" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & ⇒ & v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}-{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x){|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})]+\\frac{1}{4}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}]+\\frac{1}{4}[0-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[0]+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332761 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26e6d4ed-2a95-5b96-a81c-b22982fa0499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{12}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330291} {:answer/id #uuid "4e20740e-c6da-5ad1-a62b-85285c7d638d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330660} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6c93f5-fe12-5a2e-9367-75108b2be7a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{6}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331383} {:answer/id #uuid "e330c811-9644-5c56-b0f4-0f24b313cf8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331956} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9e871c-2c0e-518b-a4af-68c0103a8640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{6}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e330c811-9644-5c56-b0f4-0f24b313cf8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331956} :answer-field/id #uuid "872a6308-1549-5a6a-ab50-2e8d6d61c150" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4518fd0-a820-5329-a38a-c80d052e166a" :question/math-academy-id "q-49676" :question/problem "Evaluate  ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & ⟹\\,v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}+{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-[\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})] \\\\  & +[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})] \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}]+[1-\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{12}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2314e2c3-071d-5d1e-bdca-e7896981bcd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330252} {:answer/id #uuid "79f50141-9381-5317-8323-d88e90e3a8a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331031} {:answer/id #uuid "8b21378b-3425-5e03-a842-b62254df4ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331184} {:answer/id #uuid "b85d4fc1-2e09-58f6-acfa-bed34a4d5787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331579} {:answer/id #uuid "c25d6949-0735-57ba-84b8-0d9c36850ad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c25d6949-0735-57ba-84b8-0d9c36850ad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331676} :answer-field/id #uuid "abe032e0-af40-56ec-a6ec-e09e79cb742f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b98f2873-3564-5f19-a00b-95639d25a774" :question/math-academy-id "q-49682" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & ⇒ & v=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2[\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & +2[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =2[-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}]+2[0-0] \\\\  & =2(-2\\pi )+0 \\\\  & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332764 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51a96887-12f9-54a7-9f27-889811c6585e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{arcsec}(x+2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330691} {:answer/id #uuid "5be25893-006e-5120-bfc9-3385c633e5ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x+1}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330772} {:answer/id #uuid "8597e78d-6a6c-513d-99e9-421156a1013b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x+2}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331136} {:answer/id #uuid "93c370e2-9cd9-5e58-a79b-66978cc6b8b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{arcsec}(x+5)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331260} {:answer/id #uuid "c88f3648-0e88-5c0c-930e-b5d8abbd6773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x+5}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8597e78d-6a6c-513d-99e9-421156a1013b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x+2}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331136} :answer-field/id #uuid "1358753c-7bf6-5eb8-b3a1-6471cbbf4b86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85c37b78-e926-5fab-baa1-c0d49d07ba65" :question/math-academy-id "q-49689" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\sqrt{(x+2)^{2}-25}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}-25 & =25[\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25}-1] \\\\  & =25[{(\\frac{x+2}{5})}^{2}-1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\sqrt{(x+2)^{2}-25}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\sqrt{25[((x+2)/5)^{2}-1]}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\cdot \\sqrt{25}\\cdot \\sqrt{((x+2)/5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\cdot 5\\cdot \\sqrt{((x+2)/5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{15}{|x+2|\\sqrt{((x+2)/5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{|(x+2)/5|\\sqrt{((x+2)/5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\frac{x+2}{5}.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x+2}{5}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{75}{|x+2|\\sqrt{(x+2)^{2}-25}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{|(x+2)/5|\\sqrt{((x+2)/5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =15\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =15\\,\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =15\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x+2}{5})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332765 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30a39296-35ee-53cf-bfb8-6a10b78a1084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330379} {:answer/id #uuid "54c780ec-8ed0-59df-9662-49d663639bed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330714} {:answer/id #uuid "d22501a3-a221-5893-92d1-782b53f97c93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331815} {:answer/id #uuid "ee61e062-2a60-5418-91a5-bc857edab7b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332044} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9ebae5-f2cd-530f-bdd8-1769730a7359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54c780ec-8ed0-59df-9662-49d663639bed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330714} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba7992fb-af57-5bce-a970-c192ca7e1d84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a51e949-edb8-581f-9b0d-82187677b8f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-49712" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}{log}_{4}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{e}{log}_{4}⁡(x)\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{log}_{4}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\int }_{1}^{e}u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}[uv{|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)(x){|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}x(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}[e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)-{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}[e-x{|}_{1}^{e}\\,] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}(e-e+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332766 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b9d6e3b-4caa-5c28-8e8f-30c7bb36dd87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{5}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330916} {:answer/id #uuid "91af4bda-40e5-5609-b9eb-ba605b833593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-4{e}^{-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331246} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c15c98-6584-5b0a-b607-ad7fc66191d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-5{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331514} {:answer/id #uuid "dafc5ee2-645e-57ed-b348-7496484ddc62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{-4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331888} {:answer/id #uuid "f4073828-309f-5276-8d4a-23b054539de7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-5{e}^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4073828-309f-5276-8d4a-23b054539de7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-5{e}^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332100} :answer-field/id #uuid "7332043f-db6a-5dac-b73f-3e4cc396688d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7b3a70c7-8817-5fd3-b793-bb9d93329e57" :question/math-academy-id "q-49716" :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dx=-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1}^{{e}^{4}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x(-\\frac{1}{x}){|}_{1}^{{e}^{4}}-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}(-\\frac{1}{x})(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4})(-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)(-\\frac{1}{1})]+{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{e}^{4}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)-\\frac{1}{x}{|}_{1}^{{e}^{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{e}^{4}}-(\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  & =1-5{e}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332774 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4337e381-2a60-5c5c-9308-276258a90336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330554} {:answer/id #uuid "639dd384-bc8a-5ba4-914e-7b3e50785991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330837} {:answer/id #uuid "85f0701b-e023-56df-926f-7a39fc270845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331139} {:answer/id #uuid "a9341903-3233-5c3a-89b3-545d494844f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331451} {:answer/id #uuid "afc09428-01ed-5d60-b345-93b553f0092c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9341903-3233-5c3a-89b3-545d494844f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331451} :answer-field/id #uuid "d06cfba7-b789-5962-80c5-2ed56e573863" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74490665-fc5e-5bc9-a392-77f2142c3626" :question/math-academy-id "q-49769" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)} & =\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x+3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx+1=A(x+3)+Bx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3+1 & =A(-3+3)+B(-3) \\\\ -2 & =-3B \\\\ B & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+1 & =A(0+3)+B(0) \\\\ 1 & =3A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}=\\frac{1}{3x}+\\frac{2}{3(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3x}+\\frac{2}{3(x+3)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{2}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x(x+3)^{2}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x(x+3)^{2}|{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332778 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01229e8b-95db-594f-a7ea-4485e256ee35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1)+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329964} {:answer/id #uuid "22f4b600-b35f-5fd4-9f94-a5004e748675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1)+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330250} {:answer/id #uuid "2cad15ca-d489-5824-a0be-ab69392de964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1)+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330347} {:answer/id #uuid "560d35eb-35fa-5aea-a074-d45d3fccfc62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1)+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330720} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd43830-77ba-5b6c-a34b-3129f454acee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1)+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22f4b600-b35f-5fd4-9f94-a5004e748675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1)+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330250} :answer-field/id #uuid "8467a0d4-d701-5fbc-9506-20ee41dc5e58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "edbb9f4e-e89b-5064-a45b-10c2db4c2df0" :question/math-academy-id "q-49873" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x+1}{x({x}^{2}+x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the quadratic factor in the denominator has a negative discriminant:\n\n$$\nD={1}^{2}-4(1)(1)=-3<0\n$$\n\nSo, we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x+1}{x({x}^{2}+x+1)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x({x}^{2}+x+1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+2x+1=A({x}^{2}+x+1)+(Bx+C)x.\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=1,B=2,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x+1}{x({x}^{2}+x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x+1}{x({x}^{2}+x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+x+1.$ Then $\\text{d}u=(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+x+1| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x+1}{x({x}^{2}+x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1)+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332784 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bb523f6-337d-517c-a2e0-1ae2643cab5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330192} {:answer/id #uuid "475c65c1-db8e-5275-9e24-86f4e13e330a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330593} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7c503b-c042-5870-ab52-c9e84dc594fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331180} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0ffc0e-686f-5268-9425-c55b0fcb8ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331332} {:answer/id #uuid "c8512e2d-a2c6-5eea-98b8-e618ad907ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a7c503b-c042-5870-ab52-c9e84dc594fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331180} :answer-field/id #uuid "110a7998-6b53-557c-88e4-37cc3407f3a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e05f40f4-bb40-5adb-aa19-6a15bedeece9" :question/math-academy-id "q-49902" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{4x}{(x-2)^{2}}\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4x}{(x-2)^{2}} & =\\frac{A}{(x-2)}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-2)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n4x=A(x-2)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2) & =A(2-2)+B \\\\ 8 & =0+B \\\\ B & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0) & =A(0-2)+B \\\\ 0 & =-2A+B \\\\ 0 & =-2A+8 \\\\ A & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x}{(x-2)^{2}}=\\frac{4}{(x-2)}+\\frac{8}{(x-2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{4x}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}(\\frac{4}{(x-2)}+\\frac{8}{(x-2)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x+8{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|{|}_{3}^{4}+8(-\\frac{1}{x-2}){|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4-2|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-2|)+8(-\\frac{1}{4-2}+\\frac{1}{3-2}) \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4-2|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-2|)+8(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8eb71edc-1611-5574-8e7e-8f7b3936ff0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331213} {:answer/id #uuid "97f1817a-04e4-55bd-9d46-5de306b15c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331304} {:answer/id #uuid "abcc04b6-41a0-58ce-94e2-8537295db75a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331475} {:answer/id #uuid "c490eb1b-6330-524a-a5e4-555370670a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331697} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc8d2f7-7152-57db-b954-c96f6cb2f941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c490eb1b-6330-524a-a5e4-555370670a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331697} :answer-field/id #uuid "15871e44-ae83-5585-90d9-a66f6aac88ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2807863a-263b-547c-96e7-4e784ccb6278" :question/math-academy-id "q-49905" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{5x-1}{(x-3)(2x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C,\n$$\n\nfind the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-1}{(x-3)(2x+1)} & =\\frac{A}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{2x+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n5x-1=A(2x+1)+B(x-3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-\\frac{1}{2})-1 & =A(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1)+B(-\\frac{1}{2}-3) \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & =-\\frac{7}{2}B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(3)-1 & =A(2(3)+1)+B(3-3) \\\\ 14 & =7A \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-1}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{2}{x-3}+\\frac{1}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x-1}{(x-3)(2x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x-3}+\\frac{1}{2x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and $a+b=2+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bfc3304-e547-5d93-85cf-d63b46ad634d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330197} {:answer/id #uuid "945ced1f-8fea-5c1d-a75d-ca4b571df7b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331265} {:answer/id #uuid "b3053017-09aa-5c07-8524-e09690ea6055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331530} {:answer/id #uuid "bf976cda-48d5-50b8-89c7-c7fb49822dfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331648} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc85048-9121-59e1-9a1e-20b6d9412725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf976cda-48d5-50b8-89c7-c7fb49822dfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331648} :answer-field/id #uuid "54e3b30d-0abd-511a-a133-4c34869bac9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4836967-af93-5f43-b351-fdcfbef30921" :question/math-academy-id "q-49908" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{3{x}^{2}+x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+x=x(3x+1).\n$$\n\nWe convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x(3x+1)} & =\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{(3x+1)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2=A(3x+1)+Bx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A(3(-\\frac{1}{3})+1)+B(-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ 2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}B \\\\ 6 & =-B \\\\ B & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A(3(0)+1)+B(0) \\\\ 2 & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x(3x+1)}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{6}{3x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{x(3x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{6}{3x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{3}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35ceb9bc-32d1-5e15-a733-fddde56fa205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{\\sqrt{11}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330430} {:answer/id #uuid "3814f410-4692-533f-ab4d-9fc4e28c8766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{11})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330449} {:answer/id #uuid "402c58c4-6fc4-5b10-b233-048c89f390b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{33}}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x+3)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330522} {:answer/id #uuid "4edd4e6d-9d85-570a-818c-e40797a3d8e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{\\sqrt{11}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330666} {:answer/id #uuid "6fcef366-c806-5aba-853e-73a4a9c9ca91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x+11}{\\sqrt{3}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4edd4e6d-9d85-570a-818c-e40797a3d8e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{\\sqrt{11}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330666} :answer-field/id #uuid "3df9f5da-1d1a-5b9b-a71c-2f5dea7ce56e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d8b04a6-d5ee-5401-b693-4f822d6507a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-49934" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-3{x}^{2}-18x+6}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}-18x+6 & =-3({x}^{2}+6x-2) \\\\  & =-3({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+[{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}]-2) \\\\  & =-3({(x+3)}^{2}-11) \\\\  & =3(11-{(x+3)}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-3{x}^{2}-18x+6}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3(11-{(x+3)}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{33(1-(x+3)^{2}/11)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{33}\\cdot \\sqrt{1-(x+3)^{2}/11}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{33}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((x+3)/\\sqrt{11})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{x+3}{\\sqrt{11}}).$  \n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x+3}{\\sqrt{11}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-3{x}^{2}-18x+6}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{33}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-((x+3)/\\sqrt{11})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{33}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{\\sqrt{11}})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "000b0a0b-70f8-5315-a3e8-d327557b59b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{20}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329951} {:answer/id #uuid "33c26726-2c08-5c56-acf6-11b3a6d892a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=13\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330408} {:answer/id #uuid "354ea829-6b99-586e-9084-20a4da546159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=7\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330424} {:answer/id #uuid "638fdc3b-3aeb-5353-bdbb-4803c2a85748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{29}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=1\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330836} {:answer/id #uuid "bea51915-1a2e-5ce4-8e17-178a65bc2f4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "354ea829-6b99-586e-9084-20a4da546159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{\\text{max}}=7\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330424} :answer-field/id #uuid "ceff4dc0-ec20-5b88-b38d-e75183e810fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "407abfb1-af1c-5ee1-847b-8b98a1270fb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-49950" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=2-\\frac{2}{3}t,$ where $t⩾0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ calculate the maximum velocity ${v}_{\\text{max}}$ of the particle and determine the time when the particle reaches its maximum velocity." :question/worked-solution "The extrema of the velocity $v$ occur at the critical points of $v.$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=a(t),$ to find the times where $v$ is a maximum, we just need to solve $a(t)=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $a(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{2}{3}t & =0 \\\\ t & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the second derivative test. For this, we find the second derivative of $v(t)$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}v}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-\\frac{2}{3}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${v}^{″}(3)<0,$ we conclude that the velocity is maximized when $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nNow, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2-\\frac{2}{3}t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2t-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{2}+C & =4 \\\\ 0+0+C & =4\\,⟹\\,C=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2t-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{2}+4.$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{max}}=v(3)=2(3)-\\frac{1}{3}(3)^{2}+4=7\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022ef44a-f614-5f77-b7e2-557109b501db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10({e}^{6\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329981} {:answer/id #uuid "8742e199-0ed1-50dc-9302-78ef51c46417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6\\pi }-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331156} {:answer/id #uuid "8d92ec7b-74b7-5c7d-9500-11ccb93e48fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi }+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331206} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b81776-68f2-5cff-99ae-6539316265be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}({e}^{6\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331688} {:answer/id #uuid "f9bc2eda-c095-51f8-ae6e-c8565f7e54e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}({e}^{6\\pi }+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3b81776-68f2-5cff-99ae-6539316265be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}({e}^{6\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331688} :answer-field/id #uuid "1154688a-787f-51c1-bf1f-2ddfd1ba7f5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7c6d678-a2a1-540c-89d9-058ea3cefa9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-49967" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(3{e}^{3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{3x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{3x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(3{e}^{3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3J \\\\ I & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3(-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3I) \\\\ I & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-9I \\\\ 10I & ={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(\\frac{{e}^{3x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{10}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2\\pi )}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi ))}{10}-\\frac{{e}^{3(0)}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{6\\pi }}{10}-\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}({e}^{6\\pi }-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332793 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3543fea0-b938-58c1-bd00-109dd3c70eb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{\\pi }+{e}^{-\\pi })" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330423} {:answer/id #uuid "c52ac36e-14b1-546f-b07f-4bc6d5f7fd0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}({e}^{\\pi }+{e}^{-\\pi })" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331701} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c7cf65-0dd8-5a3f-a79e-bbb685f2d553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi /2}-{e}^{-\\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332002} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c0d314-e8a4-5e16-98e6-8258f95848e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi /2}+{e}^{-\\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332097} {:answer/id #uuid "faf9cf32-eafa-5676-a480-7ce065ad668a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{\\pi }-{e}^{-\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3543fea0-b938-58c1-bd00-109dd3c70eb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{\\pi }+{e}^{-\\pi })" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330423} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dfbffea-9b18-5463-a762-458d0fd6ca9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "772519fa-1384-5f3f-a9df-50e6d7ec983a" :question/math-academy-id "q-50013" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ=\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{-2x}\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-2x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(-2{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I,$ we can subsequently solve for $I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2J \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2({e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2I) \\\\ I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4I \\\\ 5I & =-{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ I & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2(\\pi /2)}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))+\\frac{{e}^{-2(-\\pi /2)}}{5}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{-\\pi }}{5}-\\frac{2{e}^{\\pi }}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{\\pi }+{e}^{-\\pi })\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332794 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "341bbdfc-0958-57c4-a8fc-adcfd2832b27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330412} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4eb11e-164a-505d-8222-3a3c8cc2aa8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330473} {:answer/id #uuid "5173e85f-be65-57f7-b4ad-50a4243824a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330686} {:answer/id #uuid "900d9ce2-2884-5941-890f-cca5011570ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331231} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a39abd-a637-559d-b40e-9331517387e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1a39abd-a637-559d-b40e-9331517387e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331945} :answer-field/id #uuid "99a69d51-520e-57bf-a0e9-67ec91b1dca0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "defadd51-e009-5740-8eb4-1acd4e92eb56" :question/math-academy-id "q-50018" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t),$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t),$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t))}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡(2t)}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{csc}^{3}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{12},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}{|}_{t=\\pi /12} & =-\\frac{1}{4}{csc}^{3}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{sin}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332796 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c2556ab-42eb-5b03-9b82-ffafb8c7bdf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6{x}^{3}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330340} {:answer/id #uuid "340e3d02-facf-523f-89ff-093dd1bcd18f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{x}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330411} {:answer/id #uuid "aae0e4e4-5033-5e88-827b-62d31ab7b6e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331464} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f11883-6eaf-55d0-bcf4-0414977256ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332068} {:answer/id #uuid "fdcec268-59b1-5bae-be4e-f748d99e9108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{3}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdcec268-59b1-5bae-be4e-f748d99e9108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{3}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332183} :answer-field/id #uuid "9815a89d-855b-59fa-9302-62052d559095" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "860439d5-1eb6-58cf-8abd-cc9242b3ec59" :question/math-academy-id "q-50044" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation below that satisfies the condition $y(-1)=2$\n \n\n$$\nx\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=6{x}^{3}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =6(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $y=2{x}^{3}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $y(-1)=2$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{x}^{3}+C \\\\ 2 & =2(-1)^{3}+C \\\\ 2 & =-2+C \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{3}+4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2738e7d6-3ec2-56da-bb09-17a33c2b70fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{243}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330298} {:answer/id #uuid "867fc1cf-c629-5977-a559-5be782a4ae5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{128}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331144} {:answer/id #uuid "9f127cdc-14b6-5ebd-8a6a-cd64704afb31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331375} {:answer/id #uuid "dec912fa-3516-5bd3-b2f6-ac8182d64e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{81}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331919} {:answer/id #uuid "e88adb0d-e96b-5e45-829e-23733498a044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{75}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2738e7d6-3ec2-56da-bb09-17a33c2b70fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{243}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330298} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0d8018d-ef9f-5fa9-bbd3-ab865b290ade" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3230dff8-85be-5bcf-b6ff-98ca4c7a98c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-50049" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}(2x+3)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{2x+3},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$, $B$, and $C$ are real constants, determine the value of $A\\cdot B\\cdot C.$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by ${x}^{2}(2x+3)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =Ax(2x+3)+B(2x+3)+C{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+2 & =A(0)(2(0)+3)+B(2(0)+3)+C(0)^{2} \\\\ 2 & =3B \\\\ B & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}+2 & =A(-\\frac{3}{2})(2(-\\frac{3}{2})+3)+B(2(-\\frac{3}{2})+3)+C{(-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =C(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ C & =\\frac{4}{18} \\\\ C & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, letting $x=1$ and using our values for $B$ and $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+2 & =A(1)(2(1)+3)+(\\frac{2}{3})(2(1)+3)+(\\frac{2}{9})(1)^{2} \\\\ 3 & =5A+\\frac{10}{3}+\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ 3 & =5A+\\frac{32}{9} \\\\ 5A & =-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B\\cdot C & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{243}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3120f347-6512-51ba-8814-9e94e5b4f3e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330382} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce95280-683d-5e12-8dc4-4bd3c4dab2ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330490} {:answer/id #uuid "3f867192-f0f3-5837-8919-076f5a3bf835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330514} {:answer/id #uuid "ed3c8989-f6d0-5647-ba9e-eebb2011d570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{4}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332030} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd989d0-88b5-5bdf-841b-6beef220f5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{4}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C{x}^{2}+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ce95280-683d-5e12-8dc4-4bd3c4dab2ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330490} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cc205ab-aa57-5042-9e55-6ce79c5b03ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d572743-1005-5db1-a849-a7030811290a" :question/math-academy-id "q-50146" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation $x\\cdot \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=6{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=6{x}^{2}-4x.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (6{x}^{2}-4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-4(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+C \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+C)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})-2(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2049d209-f71d-5036-a5ed-1c707b8cca2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{3}{8}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330227} {:answer/id #uuid "2a1e5edf-0eb9-5389-987f-0ab7fb0a9f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330322} {:answer/id #uuid "498eeb73-48b5-5b64-8c4a-7b75c33fa56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330609} {:answer/id #uuid "abb3132c-a6e4-5d7f-896f-2f57e4856624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331473} {:answer/id #uuid "b1273dac-06c9-51ed-931a-5a5528347a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "498eeb73-48b5-5b64-8c4a-7b75c33fa56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330609} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7be81ae-8223-53d0-8023-701f2e8a7fd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e8ec66f-4e06-54fb-9f1e-056dedad6cc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-50226" :question/problem "$\\int (4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4{x}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+1)(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aedc002-f5a6-54e6-9744-7dcd1264c9db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{5t-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330044} {:answer/id #uuid "94c7559f-dc37-58c5-8f05-31b0f239cbbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2+5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331267} {:answer/id #uuid "9770e4f7-8ffd-5efd-b347-5be76f613443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331298} {:answer/id #uuid "99afcf09-a4ab-59a9-a911-b1d8a5327735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{2-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331327} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b97d74-536f-52a5-baba-826d10b2969c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{5-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9770e4f7-8ffd-5efd-b347-5be76f613443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2-5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331298} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dff75f0-f423-53f7-90ad-5ea82c6cdb2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "730becc0-14c6-5133-bbd8-aaf700159913" :question/math-academy-id "q-50242" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=2{e}^{3t},\\,y=3{e}^{t+2}-5$ where $t$ is a real number. Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{t+2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3{e}^{t+2}}{6{e}^{3t}}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2-2t}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2-2t})}{6{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{e}^{2-2t}}{6{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2-5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332820 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3eee4af2-0248-5ce4-b7f6-6fee6d1a8fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4},\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330507} {:answer/id #uuid "662007e1-aa2b-54ce-b04c-f2ad08993265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330861} {:answer/id #uuid "6687452e-ac8c-536f-9456-54fd1b9619f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330866} {:answer/id #uuid "712abd91-42eb-5ec8-84bf-b6f634404a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4},\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330966} {:answer/id #uuid "8fd3b07d-2774-58fa-93e9-db174c995ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3eee4af2-0248-5ce4-b7f6-6fee6d1a8fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4},\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330507} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d43db0b-4e5d-5721-97a5-e44ef07baae7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "830e243e-5841-5ab7-af4b-a130c74364b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-50409" :question/problem "Given that $r(t)=⟨{sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,{cos}^{2}⁡t⟩,$ ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)={sin}^{3}⁡t,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)={cos}^{2}⁡t,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4},\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332821 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41aac7b0-e36a-52aa-883e-d2ddfd33ab74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,\\,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330532} {:answer/id #uuid "5e338882-ec9b-52c8-9509-458d83a50da1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}⟨4,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330790} {:answer/id #uuid "8489b988-e5d3-5081-88ab-f63b44492691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331127} {:answer/id #uuid "f89ff7dc-52c8-5018-b8e3-07b4fec5c000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332135} {:answer/id #uuid "fdfbc103-aa8d-5079-8217-c29c118b141a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}⟨2,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41aac7b0-e36a-52aa-883e-d2ddfd33ab74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,\\,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330532} :answer-field/id #uuid "f22c019a-4532-5eef-bb3c-f171fa627817" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90f83aed-c049-593e-9f58-73c0262de78f" :question/math-academy-id "q-50411" :question/problem "Given that $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t⟩,$ ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{sec}^{2}⁡t,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=2{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=2{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,\\sqrt{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f191b13-825b-5416-8838-96dcdfe09985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-4t}(4t+1)}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330218} {:answer/id #uuid "9872acd7-7eb7-50ae-9b3c-851760c20bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-4t}(1-2t)}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331311} {:answer/id #uuid "99ac4658-4973-5057-bf1a-ac90579a65ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2t}(4t+1)}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331325} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4c8a27-8293-5412-8e68-c58c6bc8b6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-4t}(2t-1)}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331741} {:answer/id #uuid "fe2c6554-4cb6-5fba-be9f-c05762bf19fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{-4t}(t+1)}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f191b13-825b-5416-8838-96dcdfe09985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-4t}(4t+1)}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330218} :answer-field/id #uuid "209fa9dc-670a-542f-8478-5c1255d77e7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "daa33906-2d77-5e9e-b519-e39e6466e412" :question/math-academy-id "q-50448" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x={t}^{2}-1,\\,y={e}^{-4t}.$ Given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-2{e}^{-4t}}{t},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-2{e}^{-4t}}{t},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{2}-1)=2t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{-2{e}^{-4t}}{t})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8t{e}^{-4t}+2{e}^{-4t}}{{t}^{2}})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{8t{e}^{-4t}+2{e}^{-4t}}{2{t}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4t}(4t+1)}{{t}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332824 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7434f6cd-5abd-5918-983c-90f901173537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467650} {:answer/id #uuid "390198b7-c54f-5cd9-9c6c-20eb14f25176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "390198b7-c54f-5cd9-9c6c-20eb14f25176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467651} :answer-field/id #uuid "9223a194-f14e-5bca-a4c3-a9be1301213f" :answer-field/key "${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468546} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d8c9699-34bd-5a5b-b1d1-7fc0cd00c967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467652} {:answer/id #uuid "32c64784-f8cf-5e6a-8345-7313e29025cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d8c9699-34bd-5a5b-b1d1-7fc0cd00c967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467652} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ec5739d-20d1-54f9-9ab8-3fc8589202be" :answer-field/key "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(2)}^{n}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468547} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0e3c250-5a95-5d66-9b29-c6400da6d824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467654} {:answer/id #uuid "c5386d8a-3465-59f6-895b-f357aefa1a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5386d8a-3465-59f6-895b-f357aefa1a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467655} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f75b307-815b-51f7-b01e-ead1c5a03e0b" :answer-field/key "${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{8}^{n+1}}{{16}^{n-1}}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29977f9f-fbdd-580d-914b-4f20cb78cbe3" :question/math-academy-id "q-50506" :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$ starts with $n=5.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{5}} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}}{\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(2)}^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{8{(2)}^{2}}{8{(2)}^{1}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{8}^{n+1}}{{16}^{n-1}}$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\frac{{8}^{1+1}}{{16}^{1-1}}}{\\frac{{8}^{0+1}}{{16}^{0-1}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{16}^{0}})}{(\\frac{{8}^{1}}{{16}^{-1}})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{16}^{0}})(\\frac{{16}^{-1}}{{8}^{1}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{1})(\\frac{1}{16\\cdot 8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series  ${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$ and ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{8}^{n+1}}{{16}^{n-1}}$ converge."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cf2a9bb-f82a-58f8-a20c-b05ede19e65a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330924} {:answer/id #uuid "bd396760-757f-5cc3-ae92-a73a9c032976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331619} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0ea34c-687a-52c0-b80d-c348d15417e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331898} {:answer/id #uuid "ee599243-e5a7-5411-bebf-da12a4e46cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332043} {:answer/id #uuid "f66fc1fd-c8f4-5efe-88c2-728e48b39059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc0ea34c-687a-52c0-b80d-c348d15417e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331898} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b79c0ce-dcc8-507c-809f-b44d117a88c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "368258e5-5111-5755-bf64-912ecff12c3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-50607" :question/problem "For which values of $k$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{kn-2}}{{9}^{n-1}}$ converge?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{kn-2}}{{9}^{n-1}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{kn}\\cdot {3}^{-2}}{{9}^{n-1}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{{3}^{kn}}{{9}^{n-1}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{3}^{kn}}{{9}^{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{3}^{k}}{9})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r=\\frac{{3}^{k}}{9}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{3}^{k}}{9}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{3}^{k}}{9} & <1 \\\\ {3}^{k} & <9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\frac{{3}^{k}}{9}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${3}^{k}=9$ has the solution $k=2.$ Now, since ${3}^{k}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${3}^{k}<9$ must be $k<2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3374134f-1ab4-5262-8e8c-f1702edc87a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{9}{16}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330403} {:answer/id #uuid "621ec133-af6e-5549-9575-a1e3e048e702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{12}{7}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330828} {:answer/id #uuid "8e09b5f1-610e-543d-a7c6-08d3a9552d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331210} {:answer/id #uuid "d50b0165-c443-5756-9dab-ed664f6ac4e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{3}{27}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331837} {:answer/id #uuid "f64e18c1-7361-5c91-a11c-cf60c4d2c4cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f64e18c1-7361-5c91-a11c-cf60c4d2c4cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332111} :answer-field/id #uuid "28655b4d-4ed3-526e-a0ba-2d5db4da6116" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee166994-93a7-5ed5-b2a0-5e87a17a6753" :question/math-academy-id "q-50682" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+x}}$ about $x=0$ is" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+x}}=(1+x)^{-1/2} & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(1+x)^{-3/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{3}{4}(1+x)^{-5/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =1+(-\\frac{1}{2})x+(\\frac{3}{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332829 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1050764e-7fd3-504b-b930-0b70731d5ce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{1}{4}(x-4)-\\frac{1}{12}(x-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330093} {:answer/id #uuid "20987c66-fd52-5b6a-8710-65a1fdbe2f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{1}{4}(x-4)+\\frac{1}{81}(x-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330230} {:answer/id #uuid "a428bfb1-2d01-5cf5-b609-dcaaca7deb17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{1}{2}(x-4)-\\frac{1}{32}(x-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331421} {:answer/id #uuid "abad7af0-f57a-57c0-a169-4b768d06243f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{1}{2}(x-4)+\\frac{1}{16}(x-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331472} {:answer/id #uuid "c734d130-e798-5c19-9d87-eacd2011be8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{1}{4}(x-4)-\\frac{1}{64}(x-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c734d130-e798-5c19-9d87-eacd2011be8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{1}{4}(x-4)-\\frac{1}{64}(x-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331712} :answer-field/id #uuid "13b85753-ad9b-50e0-a39b-df6c9c6048cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce167e8c-c6fa-5855-bad7-6c074cc092ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-50683" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=4$ is" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=4,$ we plug $a=4$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(4)(x-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(4),$ ${f}^{′}(4),$ and ${f}^{″}(4):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2} & ⟹ &  & f(4) & =2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(4) & =-\\frac{1}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(4),$ ${f}^{′}(4),$ and ${f}^{″}(4)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =2+\\frac{1}{4}(x-4)-(\\frac{1}{32})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(x-4)^{2} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{4}(x-4)-\\frac{1}{64}(x-4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "624b7117-00f8-551a-809e-5d1c3ff1debc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467692} {:answer/id #uuid "a1161ac7-f2a2-58a6-8a07-6b82fbac85a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1161ac7-f2a2-58a6-8a07-6b82fbac85a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467693} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b52ba51-dd6a-5109-ad4a-86c7ab07785f" :answer-field/key "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468567} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a95e3e6-c21e-5dba-88b4-2aa448e29ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467694} {:answer/id #uuid "c80a45eb-af40-56af-be7f-df024e54b91d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a95e3e6-c21e-5dba-88b4-2aa448e29ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467694} :answer-field/id #uuid "640dfa81-f2e1-5fca-a3e6-abf23e497d33" :answer-field/key "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468568} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52924de1-0701-5aac-9b3a-e34d33354697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467696} {:answer/id #uuid "dde7684e-a4ef-50e4-a409-ed9aeefc8f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52924de1-0701-5aac-9b3a-e34d33354697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467696} :answer-field/id #uuid "20a21870-3114-52a9-bdfe-348f688b5b7a" :answer-field/key "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1a500c68-ffb3-5162-a19f-22fd0f314af1" :question/math-academy-id "q-50971" :question/problem "Which of the following series converge?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We use the flowchart below to determine which tests to use for each series.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa7f0243efeab51d00aa9694dd018245f7ca4664cc1a5fdf4e646aebb61536e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's test each series in turn.\n\n- For the first series, we use the alternating series test. Observe that \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n!}\n$$\n\nis positive and decreasing for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, by the alternating series test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$\n\nis also convergent.\n\n- For the second series, we use the limit comparison test. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nWorking out the necessary limit, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}÷\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\cdot \\sqrt{n}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2n}{n+2}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2}{(1+\\frac{2}{n})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L=2$ is positive and finite. Now, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nis divergent by the $p$-series test. So, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\n$$\n\nis also divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\n- For the final series, we use the $n$th term test for divergence. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we have ${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty ,$ since the polynomial in the numerator grows faster than the polynomial in the denominator. Therefore, by the $n$th term test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nis divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}$ converges."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d794c3d-0f93-5e9b-859b-c340a6282610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330206} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2fc078-2b62-5d65-868e-79b45fc7931e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330500} {:answer/id #uuid "43112ebc-3e8a-5866-aa5d-6e50c43cc18b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330551} {:answer/id #uuid "d79174d3-f14b-59a5-9d4a-8261c63a8d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331855} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6ec74e-8319-5846-b8a1-18ee324765f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d79174d3-f14b-59a5-9d4a-8261c63a8d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331855} :answer-field/id #uuid "711e8728-f052-51b4-a0b9-2a8ed9eb7bba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54f3f214-d6dd-5486-9655-fb3920d2f817" :question/math-academy-id "q-51109" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡2x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=2x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡u}{2}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "590d6352-1cb2-5f07-b337-1a5ebbae468c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330748} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5d39fd-d361-5948-ac25-d41727644ddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331468} {:answer/id #uuid "b88545d6-ff7b-56de-916f-9ba100472dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331581} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6a7743-731d-57fb-8d43-9d7442d6b434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332006} {:answer/id #uuid "fa786860-e6f1-53ee-9dfb-09fcd349a209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b88545d6-ff7b-56de-916f-9ba100472dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331581} :answer-field/id #uuid "18da3dd1-3b0e-5e20-8435-771e2e44e2a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "132c7e25-b8d1-5b60-bc80-45357d89a5d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-51126" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /24}({cos}^{2}⁡2x-{sin}^{2}⁡2x)\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x={cos}^{2}⁡2x-{sin}^{2}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /24}({cos}^{2}⁡2x-{sin}^{2}⁡2x)\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /24}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{4}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /24} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{24})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f13677-df0c-5227-8bba-34d6b80ddae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330000} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9e27b7-7095-5a8b-9710-921e741f0993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330191} {:answer/id #uuid "61a315c1-7847-5d25-940f-454f224005a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330820} {:answer/id #uuid "625fb442-1570-580f-a661-15a8d06659a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330832} {:answer/id #uuid "96d16d6e-76a3-5966-8193-677ccf5c270b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96d16d6e-76a3-5966-8193-677ccf5c270b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331289} :answer-field/id #uuid "c619486b-e757-52b4-910a-62ef15d35a5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8957bff-2acf-5eaf-99ad-a3f22d63d361" :question/math-academy-id "q-51143" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+31}{(x-2)^{2}(2x+1)}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{2x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$, $B$, and $C$ are real constants, determine the value of $A\\cdot B\\cdot C.$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-2)^{2}(2x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+31 & =A(x-2)(2x+1)+B(2x+1)+C(x-2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+31 & =A(-\\frac{1}{2}-2)(2[-\\frac{1}{2}]+1)+B(2[-\\frac{1}{2}]+1)+C{(-\\frac{1}{2}-2)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+31 & =A(-\\frac{5}{2})(-1+1)+B(-1+1)+C(\\frac{25}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{125}{4} & =\\frac{25C}{4} \\\\ C & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=2$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2}+31 & =A(2-2)(2\\cdot 2+1)+B(2\\cdot 2+1)+C(2-2)^{2} \\\\ 35 & =5B \\\\ B & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, letting $x=0$ and using our values for $B$ and $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+31 & =A(0-2)(2\\cdot 0+1)+7(2\\cdot 0+1)+5(0-2)^{2} \\\\ 31 & =-2A+7+20 \\\\ 31 & =-2A+27 \\\\ 2A & =-4 \\\\ A & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nHence,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot B\\cdot C=(-2)\\cdot 7\\cdot 5=-70.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41568bc8-66e5-5b92-91c4-58bf4fc1454e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5({e}^{\\pi /6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330528} {:answer/id #uuid "54db32e5-e7f2-5e11-aad2-ade2bfba19a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330715} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d1d05c-6a03-5691-a924-a5b82477104f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /6}-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331410} {:answer/id #uuid "bd415b93-19f9-5d77-9f40-160be3444725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{\\pi /6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331620} {:answer/id #uuid "ef74e128-de92-54ef-9418-a05286dd0154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\pi /6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54db32e5-e7f2-5e11-aad2-ade2bfba19a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330715} :answer-field/id #uuid "575b490b-6032-578f-8fda-8c94315455f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0690f2a6-3e10-52f0-a935-4f8552e1024c" :question/math-academy-id "q-51201" :question/problem "![](images/80/8076c5991e4c66bb505fa1800ce3d78fab0fc115560ef59efb72e6776976c977.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=5{e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=5{e}^{\\theta },$ wehave\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5{e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(5{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{(5{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{25{e}^{2\\theta }+25{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{50{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{e}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /6}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /6}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d0374a7-aac2-5b65-b63b-48b09372f6ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨-\\pi ,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330064} {:answer/id #uuid "125bdfb5-0185-5ef4-912f-c54813535009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330107} {:answer/id #uuid "4e619186-79a8-531a-acbe-8d7c8870dff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨0,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330663} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbe43ae-4302-50f4-aad8-8fc5fdfbea38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330786} {:answer/id #uuid "a11c9277-e964-5ba1-be5a-ed1dae3ec9dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨1,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dbe43ae-4302-50f4-aad8-8fc5fdfbea38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330786} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f3c4445-717d-550d-bf87-b5e255d63938" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6043983c-7a93-58b2-8d5d-03f2c93edce1" :question/math-academy-id "q-51321" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v$ at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0-(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(1),1+(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "057e127d-c807-591d-8ab4-95e22bc9faaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330004} {:answer/id #uuid "14f48743-4662-5bb5-b53a-426fce87fd5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330117} {:answer/id #uuid "24a5e5b3-07af-51e2-9216-21fb858e7133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330262} {:answer/id #uuid "bb79a6c3-0dd4-5153-a975-22a9b4c3f6a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331602} {:answer/id #uuid "fe8e9006-4b8f-5722-ab7b-2a091cf005e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "057e127d-c807-591d-8ab4-95e22bc9faaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330004} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e03d404-348d-518f-9017-8816be664314" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4bf41725-c484-5d60-bf51-85bf60b6feec" :question/math-academy-id "q-51336" :question/problem "If $x=\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{8}{{y}^{2}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(3,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{8}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{-1}+8{y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1})+8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-{y}^{-2})+8\\cdot (-2{y}^{-3}) \\\\  & =-2{y}^{-2}-16{y}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{16}{{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2y-16}{{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y+16}{{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2y+16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(3,2),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(3,2)} & =-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{2(2)+16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{20} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332876 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29a920f7-9f20-5208-9e1d-0c6aab2b18b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330318} {:answer/id #uuid "3492e549-cc13-5db4-85c9-4d751fad6572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330414} {:answer/id #uuid "41cbb68b-da18-52b9-a043-a839e1494c4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330534} {:answer/id #uuid "b67595f7-0643-551e-8f5d-d302ee0b8c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331561} {:answer/id #uuid "f249507b-43e5-55ec-a0bf-fb51853c2209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b67595f7-0643-551e-8f5d-d302ee0b8c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331561} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5e34bfe-fc8c-5d9f-be31-ce6a63c9c697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18252347-e352-523e-8b93-4f58594c62c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-51354" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(0,0)$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-1.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1⟩.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a795cb-9f0e-5939-900e-adafa303f8bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330103} {:answer/id #uuid "586c59c4-4661-5f6d-8c14-a8719591fc01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330738} {:answer/id #uuid "72076787-7552-58b8-8ad4-00b516cbaf7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(6x-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330976} {:answer/id #uuid "98ee28e9-1737-5e3b-81c5-05141104f588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(6x-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331315} {:answer/id #uuid "f630c36b-13b8-5df3-93c0-7e5b92d1a0cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "586c59c4-4661-5f6d-8c14-a8719591fc01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330738} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d388fcd-2c84-5c86-86ee-03feb01e614c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "38a732d9-8b0e-58f3-ab88-f23eff634c61" :question/math-academy-id "q-52537" :question/problem "Rewrite $6{x}^{2}-2x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $6$. So, we take out a factor of $6$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n6({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}$: \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}x+{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{36}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x) & =6({(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{36}) \\\\  & =6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{36} \\\\  & =6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332898 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19984c41-2e9b-55a7-a17c-f6a14e8face7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330171} {:answer/id #uuid "5a19d3b2-f62f-5c65-8cbc-bf66ae76b565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330755} {:answer/id #uuid "90067c4f-ae37-5430-8ae1-5960ae9a9184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331230} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cc8021-894c-56c3-9e32-003542b0ae5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331811} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0b2fc2-d00a-5cb2-8558-20bf23e0f5e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a19d3b2-f62f-5c65-8cbc-bf66ae76b565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330755} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8576bd1-557c-5066-8b28-c3980d1e6e26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "178ea495-c20f-5cdc-a1d6-8971b012cd62" :question/math-academy-id "q-53745" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,-1)$ for the curve ${x}^{2}y=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-1) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 2x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,-1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1,\\,-1)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{2y}{x})}_{(1,\\,-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332899 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab9861f-bd24-5e05-a3df-1a270b940998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330329} {:answer/id #uuid "84c4f63f-b107-58fc-9a69-479fc30539ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331129} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a74c7f-fa05-586c-91b2-b13729730ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331534} {:answer/id #uuid "c67f73c6-f999-5750-bbec-ae12147c6bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331708} {:answer/id #uuid "f3011a9e-cdfd-5574-baad-72ca2f4d675d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c67f73c6-f999-5750-bbec-ae12147c6bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331708} :answer-field/id #uuid "8801c2b3-5109-5311-a205-3b2d57c10695" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c73f48a1-5f73-5fca-87a5-5adf2b36d0e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-53747" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ for the curve $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)=\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(2,\\pi /4)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)}{x})}_{(2,\\pi /4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45b557f6-3edb-5181-83a9-d501c8f38e3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330576} {:answer/id #uuid "4615b002-6927-5b19-8fec-0a981bf28557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330580} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea9c192-2487-5f0c-88d6-bba4bc8d15c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331372} {:answer/id #uuid "ab557b79-efa8-5221-881f-bc531ab4bbfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331467} {:answer/id #uuid "eae5ddb8-9bf5-5595-a494-19c67808b88a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ea9c192-2487-5f0c-88d6-bba4bc8d15c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331372} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0c83ec8-c729-560d-934b-2c4ab7bca24f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b7bca4e-70fe-57a8-8703-2bc1be5d133a" :question/math-academy-id "q-53750" :question/problem "![](images/fa/faa8a8e0521972fa860d474ac079405b6930df6c47fda05808fdf242d6d6922b.png)\n\nThe equation $xy=5$ represents a hyperbola. The tangent line at the point $(1,5)$ on the hyperbola intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(1,5).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,5)$  is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1,\\,5)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{y}{x})}_{(1,\\,5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-5) & =-5(x-1) \\\\ y & =-5(x-1)+5 \\\\ y & =-5x+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+10 \\\\ y & =-5\\cdot 0+10 \\\\ y & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,10).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20e24427-f27b-58e1-a535-5687071b81fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330232} {:answer/id #uuid "40cb1e6a-90d3-51f1-81e1-ce30d72daf00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330525} {:answer/id #uuid "41f3c393-cb41-5d4c-979b-630ddf43e5e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330536} {:answer/id #uuid "543a9b07-5657-5aae-9f12-b5ea7fabce5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330710} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc8c4cd-4e0f-51c5-9d3f-13e2c083c6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40cb1e6a-90d3-51f1-81e1-ce30d72daf00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330525} :answer-field/id #uuid "31c19b83-976d-5b73-a6d8-c0fb19b2c51f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b77b6e3d-1b8e-5eff-92f7-2ee055617638" :question/math-academy-id "q-54151" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x={t}^{2}+2,\\,y={t}^{4}+{t}^{2}.$ Given  that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=2{t}^{2}+1,$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=2{t}^{2}+1,$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{2}+2)=2t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(2{t}^{2}+1)}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4t}{2t} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c373fbd-d448-580e-9e69-f5e2321837d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{t}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330642} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd654ed-9ef2-5836-a969-bbd6e5d1d37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330770} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b56339-361d-5fc3-8351-4ee1bb4246cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331455} {:answer/id #uuid "d572db6e-67b4-5b17-acd7-f4c6f80401ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331843} {:answer/id #uuid "e5923c5b-2608-5670-87a4-c541cf4a47ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bd654ed-9ef2-5836-a969-bbd6e5d1d37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330770} :answer-field/id #uuid "2584732a-3f32-502d-9705-dd3a1eae011e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdfc853f-2868-50fe-a496-48e0f43e6760" :question/math-academy-id "q-54152" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}},\\,y=\\frac{3}{t}.$ Given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}={t}^{2},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}={t}^{2},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})=-\\frac{3}{{t}^{4}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{2})}{(-\\frac{3}{{t}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{(-\\frac{3}{{t}^{4}})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27a70d6f-694d-5a0e-b313-07a62fe05242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330303} {:answer/id #uuid "66f93660-94cf-53f8-a13d-3b62b6ee3cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330872} {:answer/id #uuid "bb8faefc-b8a6-5649-818c-4951e5b1b3a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331604} {:answer/id #uuid "ce143c00-0b59-5fbb-b15d-50673b390da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331778} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a621c5-d433-5e88-a939-a5dbb1568466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27a70d6f-694d-5a0e-b313-07a62fe05242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330303} :answer-field/id #uuid "9350cec8-b435-563e-8a55-cf4516cb7166" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e17b1aa-cc22-5c50-9e89-7364972255b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-54154" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x=2{e}^{t},\\,y={e}^{2t}.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{t},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}={e}^{t},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(2{e}^{t})=2{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}({e}^{t})}{2{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ is constant, we get\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{t=3}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332908 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03368465-f6c9-5432-a81e-079cce79d6ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329990} {:answer/id #uuid "545d815d-38c1-5363-9613-df1e0d53653e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330711} {:answer/id #uuid "99dee500-541d-56a0-86db-4d99f94554ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331330} {:answer/id #uuid "be9550e1-d095-5cab-9af4-5f9a8dc1a9e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331636} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d9922b-dd11-5b47-824c-a9e3371ac401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be9550e1-d095-5cab-9af4-5f9a8dc1a9e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331636} :answer-field/id #uuid "327842f2-2e8e-544e-ad91-985c3cdb707f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40be294f-6a5a-5ee0-a717-dcefdad171be" :question/math-academy-id "q-54156" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3},\\,y={t}^{5}+2{t}^{3}$ where $t$ is a real number. Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=5{t}^{4}+6{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{5{t}^{4}+6{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}=5{t}^{2}+6.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(5{t}^{2}+6)}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10t}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332909 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0720f878-f9e6-57b7-a9ad-2cafdbf9fd46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{csc}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330012} {:answer/id #uuid "60210d84-bcdd-576f-90af-4f4b4412e584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{csc}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330802} {:answer/id #uuid "6697d4e8-d25b-57a8-8cd4-582cc3f62c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡t}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330870} {:answer/id #uuid "9a34ba3f-6e5c-57fc-b2c0-b71cbd4bde90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{csc}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331333} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d5e7fb-cd5f-5e32-b78c-1ece3aded26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡t}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a34ba3f-6e5c-57fc-b2c0-b71cbd4bde90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{csc}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331333} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb631b10-44cd-5de1-b74f-0fe092fa9f31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e1d829e6-cbc8-5156-8b9c-b8c7a64d3319" :question/math-academy-id "q-54157" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t)}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3{csc}^{2}⁡t}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =3{csc}^{3}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332910 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07a75984-9452-5895-86f4-451320d425a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330016} {:answer/id #uuid "69f02701-58cc-567e-b606-fd7488ea03f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330899} {:answer/id #uuid "8013238c-2bdb-5df1-a2d2-be71f3393693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331081} {:answer/id #uuid "8348c8ca-0586-58c1-bba7-ccb3d6b6864f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331117} {:answer/id #uuid "d7af10db-9ba9-5aba-8e43-22c26fa0fecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69f02701-58cc-567e-b606-fd7488ea03f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330899} :answer-field/id #uuid "07a07624-21c5-565b-98f3-3c972e090388" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c80a94c-01f7-551a-aa91-f7d42ace55c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-54158" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x={e}^{2t},\\,y={e}^{4t}.$ Given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=2{e}^{2t},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=2{e}^{2t},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({e}^{2t})=2{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(2{e}^{2t})}{2{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{2{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e1d272-c680-5ddf-bf8f-1cf5ef2c8a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330240} {:answer/id #uuid "495f8d96-74b3-5132-b9f7-37586da7b0b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330608} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f44f11-cc85-5e21-9914-0e5cbdee3c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331428} {:answer/id #uuid "af8e591f-1645-5e67-bc31-3e18c93ba992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331500} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ccc595-7b4f-5947-85c7-b60389376f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3ccc595-7b4f-5947-85c7-b60389376f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331539} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb99eac2-3ea2-532a-af56-82af55d239a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af7b2808-40f2-53f1-87fb-a46df1ec93dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-54161" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x={t}^{2}-1,\\,y=4{t}^{5}+1$ where $t$ is a real number. Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=20{t}^{4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{20{t}^{4}}{2t}=10{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(10{t}^{3})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{30{t}^{2}}{2t} \\\\  & =15t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c3ca98b-b4e1-583e-9b95-7fb21be04c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330342} {:answer/id #uuid "ae6d19d9-5642-56a6-96d4-87c33e2c53d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331491} {:answer/id #uuid "e99ccfcb-28c9-5d81-